<task id="246" project="customer-provisioning-orchestration-r1">
  <metadata>
    <title>Per-customer Azure AI Content Safety, keyless (plan G26) — a ContentSafety account in every stamp, its endpoint carried H2a → H4b to the BFF, no API key anywhere, and no hard-coded dev-endpoint fallback in the BFF</title>
    <phase>Model 1 dedicated-stamp remediation — plan §7 T246 (gap G26; hard prerequisite of T186 per D22)</phase>
    <gate>startable now (T244 ✅)</gate>
    <status>completed</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>Modifies BFF startup (.cs, tag bff-api), stamp auth (tag auth), L2 handler outputs and tests; failure modes are a stamp BFF that sends customer prompt text to a foreign endpoint, or a BFF that refuses to boot.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>sonnet</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Every piece copies an existing, merged pattern: content-safety.bicep ← doc-intelligence.bicep (T243/T244), the endpoint output chain ← redisEndpoint (T242), the boot check ← CacheModule's isLocalLike carve-out.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>high</effort>
    <parallel-group>none</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>false</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>customer.bicep / customer.json / manifest.yaml are shared by every stamp task; touches .claude/ (constraints, CHANGELOG) — main-session-only</parallel-reason>
    <tags>bicep, auth, provisioning, bff-api, content-safety, ai, provisioning-handler, model1-dedicated, testing</tags>
    <deps>244</deps>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>
    Give every customer stamp its own Azure AI Content Safety account, reached only with the stamp UAMI, and make the BFF
    use the endpoint the stamp provides — never Spaarke's dev endpoint.
  </prompt>

  <role>
    SPAARKE platform developer (Bicep, Azure RBAC, the L2 control plane, the BFF). Follow ADR-028, ADR-032, BFF §10 and the
    provisioning constraints strictly.
  </role>

  <goal>
    A stamp deployed from `customer.bicep` has a `kind: ContentSafety` account with a custom subdomain and local auth
    disabled; the stamp UAMI holds Cognitive Services User on it; its endpoint reaches the stamp BFF as
    `AiSafety__ContentSafety__Endpoint` (set by customer.bicep on deploy and by H4b from H2a's output on every run); the
    manifest carries no `ContentSafety-ApiKey`; and the BFF refuses to start outside Development/Testing when the endpoint
    is unset, instead of falling back to `https://spaarke-contentsafety-dev.cognitiveservices.azure.com/`.
  </goal>

  <justification>
    <existing>modules/content-safety.bicep already existed (unused, key-based); the endpoint chain reuses T242's redisEndpoint path (ARM output → BicepDeployOutputs → InterStepState → PerEnvSourceCatalog → per_env_settings); AiSafetyModule already registers the named client. Shared dev serves Content Safety from its AIServices account; stamps use kind OpenAI, which cannot.</existing>
    <extension>Yes — extended every existing piece: the module (keyless + UAMI role), customer.bicep, one InterStepState property, one catalog source, one manifest setting, and AiSafetyModule's existing endpoint read. No new service, interface, endpoint, package or DI registration.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>A stamp BFF sends Prompt Shield / groundedness calls to the hard-coded dev endpoint (an account that does not exist): every scan fails open and the safety perimeter silently does nothing on every customer stamp (plan G26); D22 makes this a hard prerequisite of T186.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

  <context>
    <background>
      Plan G26 / D22: stamps deploy no Content Safety resource and the manifest has no endpoint setting, so a stamp BFF uses
      the hard-coded fallback in `AiSafetyModule.cs` (`?? "https://spaarke-contentsafety-dev..."`) and Prompt Shield /
      groundedness fail open. T246 must land before T186.
      State read 2026-10-06 (read-only):
      - `modules/content-safety.bicep` exists, used by no template (header documents a one-off dev deploy); it has
        `disableLocalAuth: false` (comment: "key auth required until MI GA" — stale: the BFF already calls Content Safety
        with MI on dev) and a `contentSafetyKey` output via `listKeys()`. No UAMI role param.
      - The fallback host does not exist: the only Cognitive Services accounts in the dev subscription are
        `spaarke-openai-dev` (AIServices) and an unrelated `website-article-agent-resource`. Dev BFF `spaarke-bff-dev` sets
        `AiSafety__ContentSafety__Endpoint=https://spaarke-openai-dev.cognitiveservices.azure.com/` + MI Enabled=true —
        unaffected by removing the fallback.
      - Demo BFF `spaarke-bff-demo` (Stopped, ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production) has NO `AiSafety__*` setting — with the
        boot check it would refuse to start on its next deploy. That deploy is T242c (demo refresh); T242c must set the
        endpoint (record it on T242c — follow-on work this task absorbs as a note, not a live change).
      - Shared prod `sprksharedprod-api` is Stopped and retired (D-12); no AiSafety settings; it is not redeployed.
      - Stamp OpenAI is `kind: OpenAI` (not AIServices), so it cannot serve Content Safety the way dev's account does —
        hence a dedicated ContentSafety account per stamp.
      - Manifest: `ContentSafety-ApiKey` (value_source from-run-parameter — a channel with no writer) pinned as
        `ContentSafety-ApiKey→T246` in `RunContextContractTests.PinnedManifestGaps` and used as the positive-control
        example there; `AiSafety__ContentSafety__ManagedIdentity__Enabled=true` literal already in per_env_settings.
      - Endpoint-chain pattern (T242 Redis): customer.bicep output `redisEndpoint` → `ArmDeploymentRunner.MapOutputs`
        → `BicepDeployOutputs.RedisEndpoint` (required) → H2a `Check(...)` + `run.InterStepState.RedisEndpoint` →
        `PerEnvSourceCatalog` `from-h2a-output:redis_endpoint` (+ generator `$script:AllowedPerEnvSources`) →
        `HandlerRunInputs` H4b input → manifest per_env_settings `Redis__Endpoint`; customer.bicep also sets the same
        app setting directly on the App Service.
      - `scripts/Verify-ContentSafetyResource.ps1` reads the account key and optionally stores it in Key Vault.
    </background>
